<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Paracas</h1><p class="st-meta">800 BCE – 100 BCE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A culture of Peru's southern desert coast celebrated for the finest embroidered textiles of the ancient Andes, preserved in hundreds of mummy bundles at the Paracas peninsula.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Paracas culture developed on the south coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, around the Paracas peninsula and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ica-river">Ica</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisco-river">Pisco</a> valleys, from about 800 BCE. Early phases show the influence of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/chavin">Chavín</a> style in pottery and imagery before local traditions took over.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Paracas society was organised in chiefdoms supported by irrigation farming and rich fishing grounds. It is best known from its cemeteries: the necropolis of Wari Kayan, excavated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julio-c-tello">Julio C. Tello</a> in 1927, held more than 400 mummy bundles wrapped in brilliantly coloured embroidered mantles of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and camelid wool. Paracas people practised cranial deformation and trepanation of the skull. Their pottery was painted with resin-based colours after firing.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 100 BCE the late Paracas tradition, known as Necropolis, merged gradually into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nazca">Nazca culture</a>, which inherited its iconography and textile techniques. The large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paracas-candelabra">Paracas Candelabra</a> geoglyph is sometimes attributed to it, though its date is uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/paracas.jpg" alt="Paracas" width="320" height="479"><figcaption>Image: QQuantum · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ParacasMap.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 BCE – 100 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century-bce">9th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Unknown local cults</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Paracas culture</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-450&amp;focus=paracas" aria-label="Paracas on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2690 2654 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Paracas lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2951 2985h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3010 2910h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M3010 2910h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Peru. <a href="/atlas?year=-450&amp;focus=paracas">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
